552
00:22:01.600 --> 00:22:03.930 line:15%
All right, let's get back to the show.

553
00:22:03.930 --> 00:22:06.250
Right on, all right. Well, let's keep things moving.

554
00:22:06.250 --> 00:22:08.140
We have so many things to talk about.

555
00:22:08.140 --> 00:22:11.250
I think we're about to really step in it now,

556
00:22:11.250 --> 00:22:12.472
because we're gonna talk about NCAA swimming

557
00:22:12.472 --> 00:22:17.472
and in particular, the controversy surrounding Lia Thomas

558
00:22:18.572 --> 00:22:21.730
and what has been transpiring

559
00:22:21.730 --> 00:22:23.580
in the world of competitive swimming,

560
00:22:23.580 --> 00:22:25.790
women's competitive swimming

561
00:22:25.790 --> 00:22:30.790
and the sort of role of transgender athletes in sport.

562
00:22:33.370 --> 00:22:35.682
And this is a very heated topic obviously.

563
00:22:35.682 --> 00:22:40.620
Opinions are pretty inflamed around this,

564
00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:42.297
which has made it difficult to talk about it

565
00:22:42.297 --> 00:22:44.610
and in any kind of objective sense.

566
00:22:44.610 --> 00:22:47.710
Actually, we even talked about it weeks ago,

567
00:22:47.710 --> 00:22:50.240
about is it something that you want to get into, right?

568
00:22:50.240 --> 00:22:53.287
Yeah, I have misgivings

569
00:22:53.287 --> 00:22:55.850
about even talking about it at all,

570
00:22:55.850 --> 00:22:58.132
because I think people have their minds made up

571
00:22:58.132 --> 00:23:01.910
about this and it's certain to alienate certain people

572
00:23:01.910 --> 00:23:04.650
or you know, set certain people off.

573
00:23:04.650 --> 00:23:07.080
But I also think it's a really important thing to talk about

574
00:23:07.080 --> 00:23:07.913
and it falls squarely into terrain

575
00:23:07.913 --> 00:23:08.746
that I care about as a competitive swimmer

576
00:23:08.746 --> 00:23:13.558
with a long history and love for this sport.

577
00:23:16.070 --> 00:23:21.070
And also as somebody who cares deeply about human rights,

578
00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:23.470
civil rights and the transgender movement.

579
00:23:23.470 --> 00:23:26.010
I have transgender people in my life that I care about,

580
00:23:26.010 --> 00:23:28.400
transgender people in my family.

581
00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:31.220
So I'm very close to this issue,

582
00:23:31.220 --> 00:23:35.689
and I feel like it would be cowardly

583
00:23:35.689 --> 00:23:37.686
to not talk about it on some level,

584
00:23:37.686 --> 00:23:38.519
as sort of nerve wracking as it can be

585
00:23:38.519 --> 00:23:41.799
to kind of unpack something

586
00:23:41.799 --> 00:23:45.870
that is so hotly debated at the moment.

587
00:23:45.870 --> 00:23:50.870
And I feel like this case is in some regard,

588
00:23:52.042 --> 00:23:53.900
like the ultimate test case,

589
00:23:53.900 --> 00:23:56.968
because perhaps it's not the first instance,

590
00:23:56.968 --> 00:23:57.801
but one of the first instances

591
00:23:57.801 --> 00:23:58.634
in which a pretty elite level swimmer decides

592
00:23:58.634 --> 00:24:03.634
to transition from male to female,

593
00:24:07.242 --> 00:24:08.075
putting them in a position to succeed on a level

594
00:24:08.075 --> 00:24:13.075
that perhaps they wouldn't have been able to otherwise.

595
00:24:14.813 --> 00:24:17.802
I mean, you came up with a couple other test cases

596
00:24:17.802 --> 00:24:19.325
that I'm less familiar with.

597
00:24:19.325 --> 00:24:22.650
Yeah, there was an athlete named Juniper Eastwood

598
00:24:22.650 --> 00:24:23.483
who won running events

599
00:24:23.483 --> 00:24:24.316
from the University of Montana and Cece Telfer

600
00:24:24.316 --> 00:24:29.120
at a place called Franklin Pierce University.

601
00:24:29.120 --> 00:24:32.460
She won the 2019 Division II national championship

602
00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:34.510
in the 400 meter hurdles.

603
00:24:34.510 --> 00:24:36.023
So, which is Division II,

604
00:24:36.023 --> 00:24:40.480
this is Division I and swimming, it's a different level.

605
00:24:40.480 --> 00:24:42.940
It doesn't sound like Cece Telfer was vying

606
00:24:42.940 --> 00:24:44.743
for the overall national championship.

607
00:24:44.743 --> 00:24:46.954
So it's a little bit different in that case,

608
00:24:46.954 --> 00:24:49.170
and it's just a more high profile case.

609
00:24:49.170 --> 00:24:50.230
And it's coming at a time

610
00:24:50.230 --> 00:24:52.623
when this issue is in the forefront.

611
00:24:54.450 --> 00:24:57.332
Really the issue is this tension between inclusion

612
00:24:57.332 --> 00:25:00.791
and fairplay and whether or not these two things

613
00:25:00.791 --> 00:25:04.826
can coexist in any kind of meaningful way, right?

614
00:25:04.826 --> 00:25:09.410
Or perhaps they're irreconcilable in a case like this.

615
00:25:09.410 --> 00:25:10.361
So with respect to Lia,

616
00:25:10.361 --> 00:25:11.194
this is somebody who was born genetically male,

617
00:25:11.194 --> 00:25:16.194
started swimming at five on the men's teams.

618
00:25:20.760 --> 00:25:22.910
And I think part of the narrative that's out there

619
00:25:22.910 --> 00:25:26.620
that's misguided is that as a male swimmer,

620
00:25:26.620 --> 00:25:28.700
wasn't that successful or wasn't that great,

621
00:25:28.700 --> 00:25:30.500
but I actually pretty good.

622
00:25:30.500 --> 00:25:32.200
One of the Ivy league's best swimmers,

623
00:25:32.200 --> 00:25:33.370
he finished second in the men's 500, 1000

624
00:25:33.370 --> 00:25:34.203
and 1,650 freestyle, the Ivy league championships

625
00:25:34.203 --> 00:25:39.203
as a sophomore.

626
00:25:41.620 --> 00:25:44.930
So when you look at them on paper, as a male,

627
00:25:44.930 --> 00:25:46.750
weren't gonna make the Olympic team,

628
00:25:46.750 --> 00:25:50.210
but still rather fast in the grand scheme

629
00:25:50.210 --> 00:25:53.245
of being a male competitive swimmer.

630
00:25:53.245 --> 00:25:55.548
Of course began to transition,

631
00:25:55.548 --> 00:25:57.390
and then during the pandemic,

632
00:25:57.390 --> 00:25:58.907
when nobody was competing,

633
00:25:58.907 --> 00:26:03.907
used that year to continue this transitionary process

634
00:26:04.190 --> 00:26:07.560
and emerged at the NCAA championships this past year

635
00:26:07.560 --> 00:26:12.560
with I think 34 months of a hormone therapy under her belt.

636
00:26:13.750 --> 00:26:16.903
And prior to that, going to the Ivy league championships

637
00:26:16.903 --> 00:26:17.736
and like slaughtering everyone

638
00:26:17.736 --> 00:26:20.000
and winning all of these races,

639
00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:21.812
which I think put her on the map.

640
00:26:21.812 --> 00:26:22.645
And finally one of the fastest times,

641
00:26:22.645 --> 00:26:26.920
I think in the country at the 200 in that championship.

642
00:26:26.920 --> 00:26:30.370
Correct, and so that really kind of put the whole issue

643
00:26:30.370 --> 00:26:32.910
on the national news map,

644
00:26:32.910 --> 00:26:37.087
and then made her sort of a hot potato

645
00:26:37.087 --> 00:26:37.920
for the right wing to kind of weaponize

646
00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:41.165
this controversy for political points.

647
00:26:41.165 --> 00:26:44.890
We've seen a lot of that lately and...

648
00:26:44.890 --> 00:26:47.430
You mean blow hard politicians trying to use something

649
00:26:47.430 --> 00:26:49.600
to make themselves more powerful?

650
00:26:49.600 --> 00:26:51.100
A little bit of that, right?

651
00:26:51.986 --> 00:26:54.650
Some fear mongering around that.

652
00:26:54.650 --> 00:26:59.650
And this is a really tricky one because on the one hand,

653
00:27:01.300 --> 00:27:05.170
it's pretty clear from a purely objective point of view

654
00:27:07.450 --> 00:27:08.334
that this hormone therapy

655
00:27:08.334 --> 00:27:13.334
that Lia has undergone does not eradicate advantages,

656
00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:16.776
that she was able to accrue by living as a man

657
00:27:16.776 --> 00:27:21.776
for the better part of her life, right?

658
00:27:22.482 --> 00:27:23.315
Or going through puberty as a male.

659
00:27:23.315 --> 00:27:26.040
Yeah, going through puberty,

660
00:27:26.040 --> 00:27:28.920
developing, you know, the muscles

661
00:27:28.920 --> 00:27:31.600
and all of that, that goes into that.

662
00:27:31.600 --> 00:27:33.650
And then 34 months of hormone therapy

663
00:27:33.650 --> 00:27:35.030
is not really gonna undo all of that.

664
00:27:35.030 --> 00:27:37.530
But what is that, just pills?

665
00:27:37.530 --> 00:27:39.530
I don't really even know actually.

666
00:27:39.530 --> 00:27:42.310
I think it's hormone therapy,

667
00:27:42.310 --> 00:27:43.660
but I don't know if it's...

668
00:27:45.014 --> 00:27:45.960
I think it's predominantly pills.

669
00:27:45.960 --> 00:27:47.960
I should know more about that than I do.

670
00:27:48.860 --> 00:27:52.160
She goes into that NCAA championships.

671
00:27:52.160 --> 00:27:54.576
And what's interesting is there was an expectation

672
00:27:54.576 --> 00:27:57.116
that she was just gonna wipe the record boards clean

673
00:27:57.116 --> 00:27:58.730
and just crush everybody,

674
00:27:58.730 --> 00:28:00.580
but she actually only won one event.

675
00:28:00.580 --> 00:28:01.413
She won the 500 free.

676
00:28:01.413 --> 00:28:02.717
Because she won that first one.

677
00:28:02.717 --> 00:28:06.488
And it seemed like it was setting up for that, yeah.

678
00:28:06.488 --> 00:28:09.416
But she didn't win any other events.

679
00:28:09.416 --> 00:28:10.249
And her time in the 500 free

680
00:28:10.249 --> 00:28:11.082
was quite shy of Katie Ledecky's time.

681
00:28:11.082 --> 00:28:16.082
She went 4:33, Katie Ledecky's record is 4:24.

682
00:28:18.680 --> 00:28:20.868
It's a pretty significant difference.

683
00:28:20.868 --> 00:28:23.213
She got fifth in the 200 free,

684
00:28:24.150 --> 00:28:28.750
and was that the only other individual event that she..

685
00:28:28.750 --> 00:28:29.693
Wasn't she in the hundred as well or no?

686
00:28:29.693 --> 00:28:31.420
I think she swam on the hundred.

687
00:28:31.420 --> 00:28:32.980
But she was never a contender in the hundred,

688
00:28:32.980 --> 00:28:34.730
I don't think, but she did swim in the hundred I think.

689
00:28:34.730 --> 00:28:36.070
Did she?

690
00:28:36.070 --> 00:28:38.283
I thought so, or maybe I'm wrong.

691
00:28:39.205 --> 00:28:40.983
So just to break this down,

692
00:28:43.130 --> 00:28:48.130
when she was a male, she was 554th in the 200 free.

693
00:28:48.530 --> 00:28:51.660
And then she went to top ranked as a female.

694
00:28:51.660 --> 00:28:55.790
And her 500 free transitioning from male to female,

695
00:28:55.790 --> 00:29:00.790
her time difference was six point or 6% slower as a result

696
00:29:02.008 --> 00:29:04.090
of that transition.

697
00:29:04.090 --> 00:29:05.330
And when you look at the differences...

698
00:29:05.330 --> 00:29:07.080
In the 500 you're saying?

699
00:29:07.080 --> 00:29:08.770
In the 500, yes.

700
00:29:08.770 --> 00:29:11.510
And when you look at the differences between male

701
00:29:11.510 --> 00:29:16.510
and female performances in that event,

702
00:29:16.510 --> 00:29:20.030
the range is typically about 10 to 11%.

703
00:29:20.030 --> 00:29:24.880
So her time difference being just 6% different

704
00:29:24.880 --> 00:29:27.270
means that you could infer from that,

705
00:29:27.270 --> 00:29:31.893
that she gained somewhere between four and 5% in advantage.

706
00:29:35.340 --> 00:29:36.660
And you're saying then in 200,

707
00:29:36.660 --> 00:29:37.607
she went from 554th ranked as a male

708
00:29:37.607 --> 00:29:40.440
to the top ranked female,

709
00:29:40.440 --> 00:29:41.892
but that's before the championships?

710
00:29:41.892 --> 00:29:44.460
Yeah, because she got fifth.

711
00:29:44.460 --> 00:29:49.220
In the 500 free, her ranking went from 65th to first.

712
00:29:49.220 --> 00:29:51.510
And then in the 200 free back to the 200,

713
00:29:51.510 --> 00:29:56.410
her time difference was just 3.76 slower

714
00:29:56.410 --> 00:30:00.104
in an event that typically has a seven to 8% advantage

715
00:30:00.104 --> 00:30:03.180
when you compare males to females.

716
00:30:03.180 --> 00:30:05.923
So there does seem to be a gap here.

717
00:30:10.100 --> 00:30:11.860
And I think it's important to point out

718
00:30:11.860 --> 00:30:15.570
that, first of all, not for a moment,

719
00:30:15.570 --> 00:30:19.724
do I suspect that Lia's intentions in transitioning

720
00:30:19.724 --> 00:30:20.557
or anything but pure,

721
00:30:20.557 --> 00:30:25.050
like there's a great sports illustrator article in fact,

722
00:30:25.050 --> 00:30:26.515
I think it's the only article

723
00:30:26.515 --> 00:30:27.348
where she actually goes on the record

724
00:30:27.348 --> 00:30:31.030
to talk about this at all.

725
00:30:31.030 --> 00:30:33.050
And when you read that, you realize like,

726
00:30:33.050 --> 00:30:34.066
this is a person who really feels like

727
00:30:34.066 --> 00:30:36.887
they were in the wrong body.

728
00:30:36.887 --> 00:30:38.910
And this transition is an attempt

729
00:30:38.910 --> 00:30:40.736
to be authentically who she is.

730
00:30:40.736 --> 00:30:44.160
Yeah, this is not a craving, lust for a medal.

731
00:30:44.160 --> 00:30:45.285
This is not,

732
00:30:45.285 --> 00:30:47.110
"I'm gonna transition so that I can be better at my sport."

733
00:30:47.110 --> 00:30:48.480
I mean, that's ludicrous.

734
00:30:48.480 --> 00:30:49.510
That is ludicrous in this case.

735
00:30:49.510 --> 00:30:52.990
That is probably the fear driving a lot of these people nuts

736
00:30:52.990 --> 00:30:53.870
that are against.

737
00:30:53.870 --> 00:30:55.070
Right extrapolating on that,

738
00:30:55.070 --> 00:30:55.903
there's gonna be a wave of people

739
00:30:55.903 --> 00:30:56.765
that are gonna transition

740
00:30:56.765 --> 00:30:58.730
so they could dominate their sport.

741
00:30:58.730 --> 00:31:00.140
There's no evidence to suggest

742
00:31:00.140 --> 00:31:01.963
that anybody would actually do that,

743
00:31:02.870 --> 00:31:03.898
but that is part of the fear.

744
00:31:03.898 --> 00:31:05.010
That is the fear.

745
00:31:05.010 --> 00:31:06.655
Issue of course.

746
00:31:06.655 --> 00:31:11.488
So her motivations being pure in that regard,

747
00:31:11.488 --> 00:31:13.440
I think it's important to recognize.

748
00:31:13.440 --> 00:31:15.100
And I think it's also important to recognize

749
00:31:15.100 --> 00:31:16.900
that she didn't break any rules.

750
00:31:16.900 --> 00:31:20.400
Like she adhered to all of the NCAA

751
00:31:20.400 --> 00:31:25.400
and USA swimming rules regarding her ability to compete.

752
00:31:26.040 --> 00:31:31.040
So to vilify her I think is misplaced emotion.

753
00:31:31.700 --> 00:31:35.391
We can have a conversation about the fairness of the rules

754
00:31:35.391 --> 00:31:38.080
that the NCAA has set up regarding this.

755
00:31:38.080 --> 00:31:43.080
But I don't think that should be aimed in Lia's direction.

756
00:31:44.400 --> 00:31:47.415
I read somewhere early on in all this before the NCAA

757
00:31:47.415 --> 00:31:50.512
that, and this came to third party.

758
00:31:50.512 --> 00:31:52.350
So I don't even know if she feels this way

759
00:31:52.350 --> 00:31:55.210
that she saw herself as a Jackie Robinson,

760
00:31:55.210 --> 00:31:56.700
and that rubbed me wrong.

761
00:31:56.700 --> 00:31:57.874
Yeah, that's a tough one.

762
00:31:57.874 --> 00:31:59.729
That's not fair.

763
00:31:59.729 --> 00:32:04.729
Yeah, I mean, it's a little bit ludicrous to compare that,

764
00:32:07.010 --> 00:32:09.063
but I don't know that that came from her.

765
00:32:10.090 --> 00:32:12.453
I don't know either, I hadn't heard that.

766
00:32:14.220 --> 00:32:19.220
In terms of the fairness versus inclusion landscape though,

767
00:32:20.110 --> 00:32:23.362
obviously we want to create a situation in sport

768
00:32:23.362 --> 00:32:26.520
where everybody can be included.

769
00:32:26.520 --> 00:32:28.520
And we also wanna create a terrain

770
00:32:28.520 --> 00:32:29.911
in which it's fair for everybody.

771
00:32:29.911 --> 00:32:30.744
And I think there is a cogent argument

772
00:32:30.744 --> 00:32:35.744
to be made that this is not fair,

773
00:32:36.230 --> 00:32:37.870
the way the rules are currently written

774
00:32:37.870 --> 00:32:40.030
and the science is still emerging.

775
00:32:40.030 --> 00:32:43.390
But to say that this type of hormone therapy

776
00:32:43.390 --> 00:32:44.299
for this amount of time will create a situation

777
00:32:44.299 --> 00:32:49.010
where the playing field is fair,

778
00:32:49.010 --> 00:32:50.870
I think we're seeing that that's not the case.

779
00:32:50.870 --> 00:32:53.320
And I'm very sympathetic to the female,

780
00:32:53.320 --> 00:32:58.070
the young women athletes who have to compete against Lia.

781
00:32:58.070 --> 00:33:00.560
And it's not a binary thing.

782
00:33:00.560 --> 00:33:01.641
Like this is a difficult thing

783
00:33:01.641 --> 00:33:05.230
because I also believe that Lia should have

784
00:33:05.230 --> 00:33:07.380
the opportunity to compete,

785
00:33:07.380 --> 00:33:08.339
but how do we create a situation

786
00:33:08.339 --> 00:33:09.172
in which fairness becomes a rubric

787
00:33:09.172 --> 00:33:14.172
by which we're establishing that fair playing field?

788
00:33:18.440 --> 00:33:20.940
